window.SPEAKER_NOTES={"Electrical Theory & Energized-Line Safety for Powerline Workers":"Welcome the crew and set the tone: this course builds from the science up to daily field discipline. Stress that every rule has a reason, and the only acceptable outcome of any job is everyone going home.","What This Course Covers":"Walk the six modules so the crew sees the arc from theory to the tailboard. Emphasize that theory is not academic — it is why every later rule works.","Learning Objectives & How to Use This Training":"Set expectations: this is a conversation, not a lecture. Point out the DANGER / WARNING / BEST-PRACTICE call-outs and that the tables hold numbers they will use on the job.","Why This Training Exists":"Ground the stakes without fear-mongering — these numbers come from real incidents. Reinforce that essentially every electrical contact is preventable with the controls we are about to cover.","Electrical Theory Fundamentals":"Transition: before we touch anything energized, we have to know what is moving in the wire and why it always wants to get back to its source through every available path.","What Is Electricity?":"Keep it concrete: electricity is charge in motion, seeking its source, using every path in proportion to conductance. Drive home that the human body can be one of those paths.","Conductors, Insulators & Semiconductors":"Have the crew name conductors and insulators on a typical pole. Stress that the body and wet wood conduct, and that air only insulates at sufficient distance — it can break down.","Voltage — Electrical Pressure":"Voltage is always measured BETWEEN two points. Use the bird-on-a-wire idea here as a teaser for the second point of contact in Module 3.","Current — The Flow That Hurts You":"Hammer the key point of the whole course: current — not voltage — injures and kills. Voltage only decides how much current gets pushed through your resistance.","Resistance, Ohm's Law & Power":"Work an example. Emphasize that wet or broken skin drops resistance and raises current, and that heat and arc energy rise with the SQUARE of current (P = I²R).","The Ohm's Law / Power Wheel — Quick Reference":"Show the triangle trick. This is the one relationship worth memorizing. Tie P = I²R back to why a small rise in fault current is a big rise in arc-flash energy.","AC vs DC":"Contrast the two. Note that AC crosses zero 120 times a second but is still lethal — never let anyone think of it as momentarily 'off.'","AC Details: Frequency, Peak & RMS":"Clarify that nameplate voltages are RMS, peak is about 1.4× higher, and that line reactance is the seed of the induced voltage we will cover in Module 3.","Single-Phase vs Three-Phase":"Most services are single-phase; the system backbone is three-phase. Mention the √3 relationship between phase-to-phase and phase-to-neutral voltage.","Transformers — Stepping Voltage Up & Down":"Turns ratio sets the voltage ratio; power in ≈ power out. Flag back-feed hard: a customer's generator can re-energize a 'dead' secondary from the load side.","The Grid: From Generation to Your Service":"Have the crew place themselves on this chain — where you are tells you the voltage, the fault energy, and the controls you need.","Voltage Classes You Will Work Around":"Most contacts happen at low voltage — it is not 'safe.' Higher classes mean bigger clearances and far greater fault energy.","Module 1 — Key Takeaways":"Recap and check for questions before we move from the energy itself to what it does to the body.","Electricity & the Human Body":"Transition: now we make it personal — exactly what current does to a person, and why the controls later are non-negotiable.","How Current Affects the Body":"List the four factors: amount, path, duration, and frequency. Stress that wet skin can drop resistance from ~100,000 Ω to ~1,000 Ω — a force multiplier.","Approximate Effects of 60 Hz Current Across the Body":"Point out the dangerous range starts in milliamps — far below what trips a breaker. The values are approximate; the lesson is the scale.","The Heart, Let-Go & Fibrillation":"Explain why we never rely on letting go, and that even a 'minor' shock can cause a fatal fall from a pole or bucket. Make sure everyone knows where the AED is.","Electrical Burns":"Entry and exit wounds can hide massive internal damage. Anyone who takes a contact gets a medical evaluation — even if they feel fine.","Arc Flash & Arc Blast":"Two hazards in one event: heat (flash) and pressure (blast). Stress it can happen WITHOUT contact — proximity alone can trigger it — so distance and arc-rated PPE matter.","Step Potential & Touch Potential":"Walk through the ground gradient and demonstrate the shuffle. Tie it forward to bonding and grounding, which eliminate the gradient inside the work zone.","Downed Conductors & The Ground Around You":"First-on-scene discipline: treat it as live, keep a wide radius, and occupants stay in the vehicle unless fire forces them out. Reclosers and back-feed can re-energize it.","Module 2 — Key Takeaways":"Recap; confirm everyone has internalized 'current kills, and it does so in milliamps.'","Working Around Energized Lines & Equipment":"Transition into the heart of the course: the disciplines that keep us out of the circuit when the line is hot.","The Hierarchy of Controls for Electrical Work":"Reinforce: reach for the top first. De-energizing beats everything below it; PPE is the last line, never the only line.","Minimum Approach Distance (MAD)":"MAD protects against flashover across air. Everything conductive counts — body, tools, boom, materials. Know your utility's table before you leave the ground.","Illustrative Minimum Approach Distances (Phase-to-Ground)":"Stress these are illustrative — always use your employer's approved table. Note that altitude and transient over-voltage change the numbers.","The Second Point of Contact — The Concept That Saves Lives":"This is the most important slide in the course. Have every worker say the question out loud: 'Where is my second point of contact?' Most electrocutions are phase-to-ground through a missed second point.","Finding Your Second Point of Contact — Every Time":"Drill the scan on a real pole top — have the crew name every second point. Glove AND cover: gloves protect your hands, cover-up removes the second point.","Insulate & Isolate — The Two Methods of Protection":"Differentiate the methods: rubber-glove (insulate, within reach, cover-up mandatory) versus hot-stick / live-line (isolate the worker by distance).","Cover-Up: Purpose & Principles":"Cover-up is secondary protection that removes the second point of contact. Cover energized AND grounded parts within reach, rated for the voltage.","Cover-Up Sequence — Building the Protected Zone":"Walk the sequence: nearest hazard first, from a position of safety, working outward — then remove in reverse, last on / first off.","Cover-Up Equipment":"Name each item and where it goes. Stress voltage rating, pre-use inspection, and using approved clips so it stays put.","De-Energizing & Lockout/Tagout (LOTO)":"Walk the verification sequence and do not skip a step. 'Test the tester' on a known source before and after testing the line.","Grounding & The Equipotential Zone":"Grounds protect YOU if the line is re-energized; the equipotential zone removes any difference of potential across the body. Connect ground end first, line end last.","Induced Voltage — The Hidden Hazard on Dead Lines":"'Dead' is not 'grounded.' Explain magnetic and electrostatic coupling from parallel circuits, and why we ground at the work site, not just the ends.","Induced Voltage — The Hidden Hazard on 'Dead' Lines":"'Dead' is not 'grounded.' Explain magnetic and electrostatic coupling from parallel circuits, and why we ground at the work site, not just the ends.","Module 3 — Key Takeaways":"Recap the controls — this is the module the crew uses every single day.","Tools of the Trade":"Transition: the gear that makes insulate-and-isolate possible — and the discipline that keeps it trustworthy.","Rubber Insulating Gloves — Your First Line of Defense":"Always worn with leather protectors. Air-test and inspect before every use, honor retest dates, and store them properly in a glove bag.","Rubber Glove Voltage Classes (ASTM D120 / IEC)":"Select a class rated at or above the system voltage. Color helps quick ID, but the printed rating governs. Same system applies to sleeves and many blankets.","Rubber Sleeves, Blankets & Line Hose":"Same voltage-class system. Sleeves when the arm is exposed; blankets and line hose for cover-up. Inspect for cuts, ozone cracks, and contamination.","Live-Line (Hot-Stick) Tools":"The hot-stick method isolates the worker by distance. Keep tools clean and dry — contamination ruins the insulating surface — and dielectric-test on schedule.","Protective Grounds & Grounding Equipment":"Size grounds for the available fault current and clearing time. Ground end first, line end last; remove in reverse. Inspect for broken strands and corroded clamps.","Voltage Detectors & Testers":"Match the detector range to the system voltage. Test the tester on a known source before AND after; test-before-touch on every conductor.","Arc-Rated FR Clothing & Personal PPE":"Rated in cal/cm² — must meet or exceed the incident energy. The outer layer must be arc-rated, nothing meltable underneath. PPE is the last layer of defense.","PPE & Tool Inspection / Test Intervals (Typical)":"Intervals are typical — follow your program. If equipment is dropped, suspect, or out of test date, it comes out of service. No exceptions.","Module 4 — Key Takeaways":"Recap: clean, dry, inspected, in-date — or out of service.","Putting It to Work in the Field":"Transition: now we tie the theory and the tools into the daily disciplines that run a safe job.","Pre-Job Planning — Before Boots Leave the Truck":"Plan scope, crew, tools, weather, access — and the rescue — before anyone leaves the truck. Know the location for emergency response.","Hazard Assessment / Job Safety Analysis (JSA)":"Break the job into steps and pair each hazard with a control from the hierarchy. The JSA is a living document — reassess when anything changes.","The Tailboard / Job Briefing — What & Why":"Cite 1910.269(c) minimum content. The tailboard is where the plan in your head becomes the plan in everyone's head.","Running an Effective Tailboard":"Walk the six steps. Engage the crew, invite input, and confirm understanding — everyone has stop-work authority.","Roles & Responsibilities on the Crew":"Define roles clearly so nothing falls through the cracks. No solo energized work — a qualified second person is present and watching.","Communication — The Glue That Holds the Job Together":"Use three-way communication for critical commands: state, repeat back, confirm. Re-brief after any interruption, crew change, or scope change.","Emergency Response & Rescue — Plan It Before You Need It":"Brief the rescue plan every time. Protect yourself first — never become the second victim. Know where the AED is and how to do pole-top and bucket rescue.","Weather, Environment & Public Safety":"Weather can stop or change the job — lightning, wind, wet, ice. Protect the public with barriers and traffic control; keep bystanders clear of downed lines.","Module 5 — Key Takeaways":"Recap the field disciplines and reinforce that stop-work authority is an obligation, not just a right.","Regulations & Standards":"Transition: the 'why it is written' behind every rule we have covered — and where to find it.","Why Regulations Exist — and Why You Should Know Them":"Regulations set the minimum; your program may be stricter — always follow the stricter rule. Knowing the source makes you a more credible, safer worker.","OSHA 1910.269 — Electric Power Generation, Transmission & Distribution":"The core standard for operating and maintaining power systems. Walk the key subsections — if a worker reads one regulation, make it this one.","OSHA 1910.269 vs 1926 Subpart V":"Maintenance (1910.269) versus construction (1926 Subpart V), harmonized on the key requirements. Use the standard that matches the type of work.","The National Electrical Safety Code (NESC / IEEE C2)":"Governs the system — clearances, structure strength, grounding, and work rules. Distinct from the NEC (NFPA 70), which covers premises wiring.","Key ASTM / IEEE / ANSI Equipment Standards":"These define how your PPE and tools are built, rated, and re-tested. The numbers printed on your gloves and grounds trace back to here.","Qualified Workers, Training & Apprenticeship":"Only qualified employees work on or near energized parts. Qualification is earned through apprenticeship and verified skills — and documented.","Recordkeeping & Program Responsibilities":"What records to keep and the employer's program duties. Records are both a legal requirement and a tool for learning after every event.","Module 6 — Key Takeaways":"Recap; remember compliance is the floor, not the goal — the goal is everyone home safe.","Bringing It All Together":"Transition to synthesis — turning everything into one mental model and a set of daily habits.","From Theory to the Field — The Mental Model":"Walk the six-step loop slowly: understand the energy, find the circuit, choose the control, verify, brief, work and reassess. This is the through-line of the whole course.","Ten Disciplines That Keep Crews Alive":"Read these as a crew pledge. If they remember nothing else from the day, make it these ten.","Final Word":"Close on the philosophy: electricity is invisible and unforgiving, but entirely predictable — which means the hazard is always controllable. Look out for each other.","Resources & References":"Point the crew to the standards and, most importantly, to their own employer's procedures and approved MAD tables.","Questions & Discussion":"Open the floor. Bring it back to their system, their equipment, and their procedures."};
